     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. 15 g/day of acetaminophen is a dose that would very likely cause acute liver injury, and I don't know of any clinical indication for it. A single 5 g dose is already close to the toxic range for a 70 kg patient, and repeating it three times a day would give about 214 mg/kg/day.\n\nIf this is meant to be an unusual protocol, there are two ways to proceed:\n- **Pharmacy or toxicology review.** They can verify the order and enter it through the hospital's override process.\n- **A different dose.** I can enter acetaminophen at or below 1,000 mg per dose and no more than 4 g/day (or 3 g/day if you want a more conservative ceiling).\n\nIf the patient may already have taken a large amount of acetaminophen, that is a poisoning scenario. It needs a serum level and a decision about N-acetylcysteine, and poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) can help.\n\nTell me the dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
